e find the largest powers for $(y-2)$ and $(z+1)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(y-2)^{5}$ and $(y-2)^{2},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\n- For $(z+1)^{3}$ and $(z+1)^{6},$ the largest power is $6.$\n\nNow, we raise $(y-2)$ and $(z+1)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y-2)^{5}(z+1)^{3},\\,(y-2)^{2}(z+1)^{6})=(y-2)^{5}\\cdot (z+1)^{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(9(y-2)^{5}(z+1)^{3},\\,15(y-2)^{2}(z+1)^{6})=45(y-2)^{5}(z+1)^{6}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf993c3-d9d9-5e72-8d2a-b4ef24b5806b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{z}^{6}(w-7)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bf993c3-d9d9-5e72-8d2a-b4ef24b5806b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{z}^{6}(w-7)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288968} :answer-field/id #uuid "316b5cc6-883c-5eea-a60b-2a81f71f9152" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37946c0d-d53c-548b-97e8-ba2b00c136c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-222116" :question/problem "The least common multiple of the polynomials $12{z}^{6}(w-7)^{3}$ and $20{z}^{5}(w-7)^{4}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(12,20):$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,\\,80,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(12,20)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of ${z}^{6}(w-7)^{3}$ and ${z}^{5}(w-7)^{4},$ we find the largest powers for $z$ and $(w-7)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For ${z}^{6}$ and ${z}^{5},$ the largest power is $6.$\n\n- For $(w-7)^{3}$ and $(w-7)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\nNow, we raise $z$ and $(w-7)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({z}^{6}(w-7)^{3},\\,{z}^{5}(w-7)^{4})={z}^{6}\\cdot (w-7)^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(12{z}^{6}(w-7)^{3},\\,20{z}^{5}(w-7)^{4})=60{z}^{6}(w-7)^{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0f49257-81d7-5f6a-b790-b8f75f1f0f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0f49257-81d7-5f6a-b790-b8f75f1f0f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518751} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e83684-a26f-520e-9f22-9e5e620c6418" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d65105c-f360-50e1-a105-3d1c246de3be" :question/problem "The least common multiple of the polynomials $7(u+4)^{2}(y-3)$ and $6(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(7,6):$\n\n- Multiples of $7:$ $\\,7,\\,14,\\,21,\\,28,\\,35,\\,42,\\,49,…$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,\\,12,\\,18,\\,24,\\,30,\\,36,\\,42,\\,48,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(7,6)=42.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(u+4)^{2}(y-3)$ and $(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5},$ we find the largest powers for $(u+4)$ and $(y-3)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(u+4)^{2}$ and $(u+4)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(y-3)$ and $(y-3)^{5},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\nNow, we raise $(u+4)$ and $(y-3)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((u+4)^{2}(y-3),\\,(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5})=(u+4)^{4}\\cdot (y-3)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(7(u+4)^{2}(y-3),\\,6(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5})=42(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "337031ce-cfce-5412-b4ce-3bf42817e30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "337031ce-cfce-5412-b4ce-3bf42817e30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518723} :answer-field/id #uuid "edfd4280-37d7-59ae-9f16-cc68c56e420a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b03d1f0-fd10-5108-8d61-995f78e9f43f" :question/problem "The least common multiple of the polynomials $18(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}$ and $16(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{8}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(18,16):$\n\n- Multiples of $18:$ $\\,18,\\,36,\\,54,\\,72,\\,90,\\,108,\\,126,\\,144,\\,162,…$\n\n- Multiples of $16:$ $\\,16,\\,32,\\,48,\\,64,\\,80,\\,96,\\,112,\\,128,\\,144,\\,160,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(18,16)=144.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}$ and $(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{8},$ we find the largest powers for $(w+6)$ and $(z-4)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(w+6)^{4}$ and $(w+6)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(z-4)^{9}$ and $(z-4)^{8},$ the largest power is $9.$\n\nNow, we raise $(w+6)$ and $(z-4)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9},\\,(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{8})=(w+6)^{4}\\cdot (z-4)^{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(18(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9},\\,16(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{8})=144(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bdb3e46-73c5-57dc-9f72-d9f33bccbd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168{z}^{8}(w-1)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518728} {:answer/id #uuid "50e88fcd-399b-55b2-a76d-293fbd039f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168{z}^{6}(w-1)^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518730} {:answer/id #uuid "5f73f56d-80e6-58ad-a366-c1d98cd7169a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{6}(w-1)^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518734} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4db854-5e8d-5767-81e5-397a0c949b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168{z}^{9}(w-1)^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518737} {:answer/id #uuid "c16d897e-e3ee-5430-9009-bd25dd242180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168{z}^{3}(w-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50e88fcd-399b-55b2-a76d-29